Presidential elections were held in [[Benin]] in March 1991, the first direct presidential elections since [[1970 Dahomeyan presidential election|1970]]. Elections under the military regime of [[Mathieu Kérékou]] had been indirect, with the president chosen by the National Assembly.

The first round, held on 10 March, saw no candidate receive more than 37% of the vote.  Prime Minister [[Nicéphore Soglo]] led the field, with Kérékou in second place.  The second round on 24 March resulted in a decisive victory for Soglo, with almost two-thirds of the vote. Voter turnout was 56% in the first round and 64% in the second.<ref>[[Dieter Nohlen]], Michael Krennerich & Bernhard Thibaut (1999) ''Elections in Africa: A data handbook'', p94 {{ISBN|0-19-829645-2}}</ref>

The elections marked the first instance in post-colonial Francophone Africa that an opposition candidate won a free election.

